# Build output
/dist/
/apps/desktop/out/
/node_modules/
/apps/desktop/node_modules/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Hers, not the repository's.
#
# config.toml holds the OBS password, so it never goes in; config.example.toml
# is the template. data/ holds her logs, her YouTube token, her API keys and
# the downloaded speech models.
#
# The command files are NOT ignored: the phrases are part of the product, the
# tests load them, and a fresh clone needs them to have any commands at all.
# Editing them will show up as a diff, which is the right trade.
/config.toml
/data/

# Wake-word training: gigabytes of borrowed audio and the environment that
# processes it. All of it is reproducible from tools/wakeword, and the one file
# worth keeping -- the trained model -- is committed beside the code that loads
# it.
/.wakeword/
/tools/wakeword/.venv/

# Credentials, which never belong in a public repository.
#
# .npmrc carries an npm auth token.
#
# The OAuth client reaches a release build from two GitHub secrets, sealed in
# at link time, and never from a file in this tree -- a secret in a source tree
# is scraped within hours, and Google revokes the client rather than the copy
# that leaked, which would break the sign-in for everyone at once, including
# her, mid-stream. The names below are the shapes that mistake takes; ignoring
# them means a slip is not one `git add .` away from being permanent.
/.npmrc
client_secret*.json
*.p12
*.pem

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
/tools/wakeword/__pycache__/

# Scratch from the tools around the work, not the work.
#
# Logs an agent or the browser driver left behind, a captured engine run, and
# one-off binaries built to answer a question. None of it rebuilds anything and
# .trwatch alone is several megabytes, which is a poor thing to carry forever
# in a repository this small.
/.omo/
/.playwright-mcp/
/.trwatch/
/score.exe
/we.txt
/wo.txt
